staying overseas and to Nepal we go now  where hundreds of Rescuers are still  searching for survivors after a plane  crash that killed at least 68 people  Searchers found the plane's voice and  data recorders this morning four people  still unaccounted for in the rubble  there after yesterday's crash and police  say hopes of finding anyone alive are  fading Elizabeth Palmer has more now on  Nepal's deadliest accident in more than  30 years  in the evening another body journeyed by  ambulance from the crash site to a  hospital where loved ones waited hours  earlier a phone camera had caught the  plane flying low then abruptly tilting  before it hit the ground  it nose dived into a Gorge near Pokhara  a town popular with both Himalayan  Trekkers and pilgrims onlookers rushed  to the crash site while Rescuers  including the Nepali military scoured  the wreckage for signs of life but there  were none this is Nepal's worst civil  aviation disaster in 30 years the yeti  Airlines twin-engine turboprop had begun  what air traffic control said was a  normal approach to the brand new Pokhara  airport when something went disastrously  wrong now an investigation will try to  determine what that was most of those on  board were Nepali but included  passengers from Australia Ireland France  India Russia and South Korea Nepal has a  deadly air safety history nearly 350  people have been killed in crashes since  2000 including the most lethal one in  1992 where all 167 people on board the  plane died for CBS mornings I'm  Elizabeth Palmer in Tokyo
 

